bedding-specific ranks and refresh\nschedules, yielding additional communication and memory savings over keeping\nembeddings dense. Across pretraining from 60M to 1B model scales, TSR-Adam\nreduces average communicated bytes per step by 13×, and on GLUE fine-tuning\nit reduces communication by 25×, while achieving comparable performance; we\nfurther provide a theoretical stationarity analysis for the proposed update. Code is\navailable at https://github.com/DKmiyan/TSR-Adam.\n1\nIntroduction\nLarge-scale pretraining has established itself as the cornerstone of modern foundation models Brown\net al. [2020], Bommasani [2021], Touvron et al. [2023]. However, practical training at these scales\nrelies heavily on distributed data-parallel execution, where synchronizing gradients across many accel-\nerators can become a substantial bottleneck. This limitation is exacerbated by the disparity between\n∗equal contribution.\n†Corresponding author.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: From O(mn) to O(r<sup>2</sup>): Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency. (https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2602-08007.html)\nciteturn31580search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # \"From O(mn) to O(r2): Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam ...\" ... type: Informal or Other Publication ... CoRR abs/2602.08007 (2026)\n\n# \"From O(mn) to O(r2): Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam ...\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nSizhe Dang et al. (2026)\n\n\nDOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2602.08007\n\naccess: openImage\n\ntype: Informal or Other Publication\n\nmetadata version: 2026-03-25\n\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n    * view\n\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2602-08007\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2602-08007\n\nSizhe Dang, Jiaqi Shao, Xiaodong Zheng, Guang Dai, Yan Song, Haishan Ye:\nFrom O(mn) to O(r^{2}): Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency. CoRR abs/2602.08007 (2026)\n\n\nlast updated on 2026-03-25 08:13 CET by the dblp team--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistributed Training - Domain Research Papers - Arxiv Search - Page 5 | Arxiv - DeepPaper (https://arxiv.deeppaper.ai/domain_tags/Distributed%20Training?locale=en&page=5)\nciteturn31580search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Mar 09, 2026 · Image: arXiv 2603.08163v2 ... ## From $O(mn)$ to $O(r^2)$: Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency ... This paper proposes TSR-Adam, a novel two-sided low-rank communication method for Adam-family optimizers in distributed training, significantly reducing communication costs while maintaining performance.\n\nKey Contributions:\n\nThe paper empirically identifies and analyzes network and fabric-related failure modes in distributed GPU training at scale, providing practical diagnostic principles to improve scaling predictability and reduce costs.\n\nDL Distributed Training System Optimization Benchmark application\n\n## From $O(mn)$ to $O(r^2)$: Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency\n\nFeb 08, 2026 · Image: arXiv 2602.08007v1\n\nThis paper proposes TSR-Adam, a novel two-sided low-rank communication method for Adam-family optimizers in distributed training, significantly reducing communication costs while maintaining performance.\n\nKey Contributions:\n\nThe key contribution is the introduction of TSR-Adam, a two-sided low-rank communication method that reduces the communication payload from O(mn) to O(r^2) for Adam-family optimizers in distributed training, along with a randomized SVD-based refresh to further reduce peak communication.\n\nDL Optimization Distributed Training method Theory\n\n## Mitigating Staleness in Asynchronous Pipeline Parallelism via Basis Rotation\n\nFeb 03, 2026 · Image: arXiv 2602.03515v1\n\nThis paper proposes a basis rotation method to mitigate gradient staleness in asynchronous pipeline parallelism, improving convergence and scalability.\n\nKey Contributions:\n\nThe key contribution is the identification of Hessian eigenbasis misalignment as a major cause of slow convergence in asynchronous pipeline parallelism and the proposal of basis rotation to address this issue, leading to significant performance improvements.\n\nML DL Distributed Training method Theory--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom O(mn) to O(r2): Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/from-o-mn-to-o-r-2-two-sided-low-rank-communication-for-adam-in-distributed/wrk_71fbe563d3ea14abf444fec574acc262)\nciteturn31580search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # From $O(mn)$ to $O(r^2)$: Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency ... arXiv 2026 ... TSR-Adam introduces a two-sided low-rank communication framework that slashes the communication payload of distributed LLM training from $O(mn)$ to a dimension-independent $O(r^2)$.\n\n# From $O(mn)$ to $O(r^2)$: Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency\n\nSizhe Dang, Jiaqi Shao, Xiaodong Zheng, Guang Dai, Yan Song, Haishan Ye\n\narXiv 2026\n\nRead Paper Bookmark\n\n## TL;DR\n\nTSR-Adam introduces a two-sided low-rank communication framework that slashes the communication payload of distributed LLM training from $O(mn)$ to a dimension-independent $O(r^2)$. By synchronizing a tiny core matrix and utilizing randomized SVD for subspace refreshes, it reduces average communication bytes by up to $24\\times$ during pretraining and $25\\times$ during fine-tuning, all while maintaining memory efficiency and comparable model performance.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nAs foundation models scale to billions of parameters, training them requires distributing the workload across massive clusters of accelerators (GPUs/TPUs). In these data-parallel setups, synchronizing gradients across nodes via network links becomes a severe bottleneck, often leaving expensive compute units idling while waiting for communication to finish.\n\nTo tackle this, researchers previously developed projection-based low-rank optimizers like GaLore. However, these methods were primarily designed for memory efficiency rather than communication efficiency. They rely on one-sided projections, meaning the synchronized object size still scales with one of the large matrix dimensions ($O(rn)$ for an $m \\times n$ matrix). Furthermore, these methods periodically require a full-gradient synchronization step to refresh their low-rank subspaces, creating massive communication spikes. Finally, they typically leave embedding layers completely dense, which dominates the communication footprint in many model configurations.\n\n## Methods\n\nThe authors propose TSR-Adam (Two-Sided Low-Rank Adam), which reframes low-rank optimization as a communication-first mechanism.\n\nImage: Comparison of communication mechanisms between dense Adam, GaLore, and the proposed TSR-Adam.Expand\n\nExtracted figure from page 2 of From $O(mn)$ to $O(r^2)$: Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency\n\nIts core components include:\n\n  1. Two-Sided Core Synchronization: Instead of projecting from just one side, TSR-Adam maintains two orthonormal bases, $U \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times r}$ and $V \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times r}$. At each step, workers compute their local gradient $G$, compress it into a tiny \"core\" matrix $C = U^\\top G V \\in \\mathbb{R}^{r \\times r}$, and perform an all-reduce only on this core.\n  2. Core-Space Adam Updates: The first and second moments of the Adam optimizer are maintained entirely within this low-dimensional $r \\times r$ core space, preserving the memory-saving benefits of low-rank training.\n  3. Randomized SVD Subspace Refresh: To prevent the massive communication spikes caused by traditional exact SVD subspace updates, TSR-Adam uses a sketch-based randomized SVD (rSVD). Workers communicate only low-dimensional sketches to update $U$ and $V$, avoiding full-gradient synchronization entirely.\n  4. Embedding-Aware Compression: TSR-Adam explicitly extends low-rank communication to embedding layers, applying embedding-specific ranks ($r_{emb}$) and separate refresh schedules ($K_{emb}$) to maximize savings.\n\n## Datasets\n\nThe authors evaluated their approach across two primary paradigms:\n\n  * Pretraining: Evaluated on the C4 (Common Crawl) dataset using LLaMA-based architectures scaled from 60M, 130M, 350M, up to 1B parameters.\n  * Fine-tuning: Evaluated on the GLUE benchmark (including tasks like CoLA, STS-B, MRPC, RTE, SST2, MNLI, QNLI, and QQP) utilizing a pretrained RoBERTa-Base model.\n\n## Key Insights and Ideas\n\n  * Dimension Independence: Moving from one-sided ($O(rn)$) to two-sided ($O(r^2)$) projection completely decouples the per-step communication payload from the actual layer dimensions of the model. This makes it uniquely future-proof as model layers grow wider.\n  * Flattening the Communication Spikes: In previous low-rank methods, the periodic cost of recalculating the subspace bases via standard SVD wiped out a large portion of the communication savings. Using randomized sketches ensures that even the \"refresh steps\" remain highly communication-efficient.\n  * Embeddings are Too Big to Ignore: While linear layers make up the bulk of compute, embedding layers often dominate gradient traffic in small-to-medium models. Treating embeddings with specialized low-rank schedules yields massive hidden communication benefits.\n\n## Key Results\n\n  * Massive Communication Reductions: Across pretraining scales, TSR-Adam reduced the average communicated bytes per step by $13\\times$ on average (and up to $24\\times$ on specific configurations) compared to standard AdamW.\n  * Fine-Tuning Efficiency: On the GLUE benchmark, TSR-Adam achieved a $25\\times$ reduction in communication volume compared to Adam, while suffering an average task performance drop of only 0.86%. It also outperformed GaLore on 6 out of 8 tasks while using $8\\times$ fewer bytes per step.\n  * Better Bytes-to-Loss Convergence: When plotting training loss against the cumulative number of bytes transmitted, TSR-Adam reaches a lower loss much faster than both AdamW and GaLore under a fixed communication budget.\nImage: Training loss vs. cumulative communicated bytes for LLaMA-60M, showing TSR-Adam's superior communication efficiency.Expand\n\nExtracted figure from page 2 of From $O(mn)$ to $O(r^2)$: Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\n  * Slight Loss Degradation: In some pretraining runs (e.g., LLaMA-60M), TSR-Adam exhibits a slightly higher final evaluation loss compared to dense AdamW (3.61 vs 3.53), showing a minor trade-off between extreme communication compression and absolute convergence quality.\n  * Theoretical Simplifications: The provided theoretical stationarity analysis is proven for a momentum SGD variant (TSR-SGD) rather than the fully adaptive TSR-Adam, as analyzing two-sided projections with adaptive learning rates remains notoriously difficult.\n  * Hyperparameter Sensitivity: The performance of the model relies on choosing appropriate ranks ($r$) and refresh intervals ($K$). Automating or dynamically scheduling these hyperparameters during training remains an open area for future exploration.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRethinking LoRA for Data Heterogeneous Federated Learning: Subspace and State Alignment (https://www.connectedpapers.com/main/902d398a6111911f364ccdf465214fc8c389c0e0)\nciteturn31580search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: today; 2026 ... From O(mn) to O(r2): Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency ... Communication-Efficient and Accurate Approach for Aggregation in Federated Low-Rank Adaptation\nJieming Bian, Lei Wang, Letian Zhang, Jie Xu\n\nOne Initialization to Rule them All: Fine-tuning via Explained Variance Adaptation\n\nF. Paischer, Lukas Hauzenberger, Thomas Schmied, Benedikt Alkin, M. Deisenroth, Sepp Hochreiter\n\nScaLoRA: Optimally Scaled Low-Rank Adaptation for Efficient High-Rank Fine-Tuning\n\nYilang Zhang, Xiaodong Yang, Yiwei Cai, Georgios B. Giannakis\n\nFrom O(mn) to O(r2): Two-Sided Low-Rank Communication for Adam in Distributed Training with Memory Efficiency\n\nSizhe Dang, Jiaqi Shao, Xiaodong Zheng, Guang Dai, Yanke Song, Haishan Ye\n\nParaBlock: Communication-Computation Parallel Block Coordinate Federated Learning for Large Language Models\n\nYujia Wang, Yuanpu Cao, Jinghui Chen\n\nFOAM: Blocked State Folding for Memory-Efficient LLM Training\n\nZiqing Wen, Jia-Huan Wang, Ping Luo, Dongsheng Li, Tao Sun\n\nCommunication-Efficient and Accurate Approach for Aggregation in Federated Low-Rank Adaptation\n\nLe-Tuan Nguyen, Minh-Duong Nguyen, Seon-Geun Jeong, Dung D. Le, Quoc-Viet Pham\n\nADF-LoRA: Alternating Low-Rank Aggregation for Decentralized Federated Fine-Tuning\n\nXiao-Yu Wang, Xiaotian Li, Zhixiang Zhou, Chen Li, Yong Liu\n\nRavan: Multi-Head Low-Rank Adaptation for Federated Fine-Tuning\n\nArian Raje, Baris Askin, Divyansh Jhunjhunwala, Gauri Joshi\n\nTraining-time Neuron Alignment through Permutation Subspace for Improving Linear Mode C